  <label...>
</div>
<div class="govuk-radios__conditional ... id="conditional-reminder-2">...</div>

Then divider, then optout-radios group.

So tab order goes through inputs only? Radio inputs are focusable via tab? Usually yes. ArrowDown navigation within a radiogroup works via arrow keys moving focus between radios regardless of intervening elements. However the script may affect behavior.

Observed failure: At step 2 (first ArrowDown?), actually the failed step index 2 corresponds to second ArrowDown? Let's look at observed.failedSteps array length 1 entry with index:2. It says title: "Move to the opt-out answer". So after pressing ArrowDown twice (starting from #reminder, first ArrowDown goes to #reminder-2 (Text message), second ArrowDown goes to #reminder-none (opt-out)). The assertion expects that after moving to opt-out, the conditional-reminder-2 (mobile number field) should be hidden (visible false). But observed visible true (still visible). So the bug: When moving from Text message radio to opt-out radio via arrow down, the conditional field for text message (mobile number) remains visible instead of being hidden.

Why? Because the radios component only updates aria-expanded and toggles hidden class on click events, not on keyboard arrow navigation. The component listens for click events on the module, and when a radio with aria-controls is clicked, it calls setAttributes which updates aria-expanded and toggles hidden class based on checked state.

But arrow key navigation changes the checked state via browser's native radio group behavior, but does not trigger a click event. Therefore the component does not update the conditional visibility.

Thus fix: Need to also handle change events (or keyup?) on radios to update attributes when the checked state changes via keyboard.

Simplest: Add event listener for 'change' on the module (or on inputs) and call setAttributes for the changed input if it has aria-controls.

Alternatively, we could modify handleClick to also handle change events. Since change fires when the checked state changes via any user interaction (click, keyboard, etc.). We'll add listener for 'change' and call setAttributes on the target if it's a radio with aria-controls.

